Also known as fibrous histiocytoma or sclerosing haemangioma.  Also known as fibrous histiocytoma or sclerosing haemangioma.  The localized proliferation of fibroblasts is probably triggered by minor trauma such as an insect bite. These benign growths are common in young women. If you can make a confident diagnosis, no treatment is required—excision will merely leave another scar. Multiple dermatofibromas have been reported in conditions such as HIV infection, SLE, and other diseases in which the immune state is altered.  The localized proliferation of fibroblasts is probably triggered by minor trauma such as an insect bite. These benign growths are common in young women. If you can make a confident diagnosis, no treatment is required—excision will merely leave another scar. Multiple dermatofibromas have been reported in conditions such as HIV infection, SLE, and other diseases in which the immune state is altered.  What should I look for?  What should I look for?  •    A brownish red dermal nodule, $0.5{-}1mathsf{c m}$ in diameter, usually on the arm, shoulder, thigh, or leg. •    A brownish red dermal nodule, $0.5{-}1\mathsf{c m}$ in diameter, usually on the arm, shoulder, thigh, or leg.

